Day 3 — Jenkins Dashboard & First Job



1. Jenkins Dashboard

Explain the main sections students see after logging into Jenkins:

Dashboard — Central place to manage Jenkins.

Jobs — All Jenkins projects/jobs.

Builds — History of job executions.

Workspace — Directory where Jenkins performs the job's work.

Console Output — Logs generated during a build.

Artifacts — Files saved from a successful build.

Credentials — Securely stored passwords, tokens, SSH keys, etc.

Nodes — Jenkins machines/agents used to execute jobs.

Plugins — Extend Jenkins functionality.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Create Your First "Hello Jenkins" Job


Go to:

	Jenkins Dashboard → New Item → Freestyle project

Job name:

	hello-jenkins

	Select Freestyle project → OK.

Under Build Steps:

	Add build step → Execute shell


	Use:

		echo "Hello Jenkins!"
		echo "Welcome to Jenkins CI/CD"
		echo "Current user:"
		whoami

		echo "Current directory:"
		pwd

		echo "Files in workspace:"
		ls -la


Click Save → Build Now.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Inspect the Build

	After the build completes:

		hello-jenkins → Build #1 → Console Output

You should see output similar to:

	Hello Jenkins!
	Welcome to Jenkins CI/CD
	Current user:
	jenkins

Current directory:

	/var/lib/jenkins/workspace/hello-jenkins

Files in workspace:
...
	Finished: SUCCESS


Console Output is one of the most important places for troubleshooting Jenkins builds.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Understand the Workspace

	Inside the job workspace, Jenkins executes commands and creates files.

	You can demonstrate:

	echo "This file was created by Jenkins" > hello.txt
	cat hello.txt
	ls -l



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------




5. Introduce Artifacts

	Add another build step:

	echo "Build completed successfully" > build-info.txt

Then configure:

	Post-build Actions → Archive the artifacts

Artifact:

	build-info.txt

	Run the job again.

	Now students can see:

		Build #1 → Artifacts → build-info.txt

Explain the difference:

	Workspace → temporary working area used during the build.

Artifact → file intentionally archived by Jenkins for later use/download.


